      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I was just using a bit of creative license to make the concept more relatable. You see, when it comes to cooling cooked poultry, it's a bit like navigating a narrow, winding path through a dense forest - you need to stay focused and avoid any obstacles that might slow you down.\n\nNow, back to the technical requirements. During the 2-6 hour window, it's essential to maintain a consistent cooling rate to prevent bacterial growth. The USDA recommends cooling cooked poultry to a temperature range of 40°F (4°C) to 45°F (7°C) during this period. This is like a steady, gentle stream that helps to wash away any bacteria that might be lurking on the surface of the poultry.\n\nTo achieve this, you can use a combination of air cooling and water cooling, such as using a plate cooler or a cold water bath. The key is to maintain a consistent temperature and airflow to prevent hot spots and bacterial growth. It's a bit like a game of \"chase the thermometer\" - you need to keep an eye on the temperature and adjust your cooling strategy as needed to stay within the safe range.\n\nAs for the specific temperature ranges, here are some general guidelines:\n\n* 2-4 hours:",
